// Weathering for the CLASSIC motif's `mud` STRATUM -- backlog #45 step 4. Selected by the band's
// `name` in motif_config/classic/strata.json: PieceProcessors looks for
// dungeons2:<motif>_<stratum>_weathering and falls back to dungeons2:<motif>_weathering.
//
// THIS FILE REPLACES classic_weathering.json ON THIS DEPTH, it does not extend it. That is forced
// by the prefab half: a pool element names exactly ONE processor_list, so a stratum's pool JSON
// names this file and the motif's never runs. Composing them here would give a procedural room
// and the prefab beside it different rules from the same pair of files.
//
// The consequence is the duplication below: dungeons2:decoration and the #10 dungeons2:spawner
// marker are COPIED VERBATIM from classic_weathering.json, because they are not weathering and a
// stratum has no reason to differ on them -- but a list that silently inherited a spawner it
// never named would be worse than a copy. Keep them in step by hand; #47 rewrites both files.
//
// PROBABILITIES ARE CONDITIONAL, NOT ABSOLUTE, exactly as in classic_weathering.json -- read that
// file's header for the arithmetic. It does not bite here, because mud_bricks has ONE chain.
//
// Only processors vetted for a procedural piece belong here; see PieceProcessors and
// WeatheringProcessorListTest.
{
  "processors": [
    {
      // dungeons2:surface_aging -- the SURFACE-SCOPED cousin of dungeons2:aging, and Dungeons2's
      // own rather than GottschCore's. Same chains, same conditional probabilities, plus one field:
      // each rule names the `surface` it applies to.
      //
      // WHY THIS BAND NEEDS IT. Its floor is cobblestone and its walls are mud brick, so the two
      // want different decay. Keying on the block alone would work today and break later: a rule on
      // minecraft:cobblestone applies wherever cobblestone is, and an all-cobble fortified room on
      // this band would have its WALLS eaten by the floor rule, silently. `surface` is the durable
      // key; block identity is a coincidence of what has been authored so far.
      //
      // THERE IS NO dungeons2:aging ENTRY IN THIS FILE, AND THAT IS LOAD-BEARING. A processor list
      // is chained -- each processor sees the previous one's output -- so a surface-gated processor
      // sitting NEXT TO an ungated one is additive, not exclusive: the ungated rules would still
      // run over the floor and a cell could decay twice on two schedules. The gates only partition
      // the piece if every rule in the file carries one. StratumWeatheringListTest pins that, because a
      // doubly-aged floor just looks like a floor with a slightly wrong rate.
      //
      // `surface` is optional and defaults to `any`, so an ungated rule here behaves exactly as
      // dungeons2:aging would. Every rule below states one on purpose.
      "processor_type": "dungeons2:surface_aging",
      // 4. A chain applies min(agings, stages), so a cap below the longest chain truncates it
      // SILENTLY -- the last stage simply never fires and the file still reads as though it does.
      // Raise this whenever a stage is added; StratumWeatheringListTest#theAgingCapCanReachTheEnd
      // OfTheChain is what catches forgetting to.
      "agings": 4,
      "rules": [
        // ===================================================================================
        // FLOOR -- relative Y 0, and in a pitted room that is the PIT's floor (see PieceSurface).
        //
        // The paving frets rather than dissolves: cobble goes mossy first, then breaks up. Rubble
        // is dungeonblocks' non-falling gravel look-alike -- real gravel in a ceiling falls and
        // litters the room below, which is why nothing here uses it (backlog #15).
        //
        //     cobblestone -> mossy_cobblestone -> rubble -> dirt
        //
        // Composed rates, net of the next stage having fired instead -- the convention
        // classic_weathering.json quotes:
        //
        //     mossy  22.5%      rubble  5.6%      dirt  1.9%
        //
        // THE FLOOR MAY OPEN TOO, as of 2026-08-29 (Mark). This said NO AIR STAGE ON A FLOOR until
        // then, on the grounds that a hole in a wall is the look while one underfoot opens onto
        // whatever terrain the dungeon was carved from. Mark's call is that a few are fine, the
        // same call he made for the corridor arch on the same day: the harm those bars were really
        // guarding against was gravel FALLING through the hole and littering the floor, and that
        // cause went away when dungeonblocks:rubble replaced gravel as the deep terminus on
        // 2026-08-25. Water finding its way in is accepted.
        //
        // Only the DRY route carries it, and only at its third stage -- 0.56% of floor cobblestone,
        // pinned by StratumWeatheringListTest#theFloorWearsOnItsOwnSchedule so that moving it is a
        // decision rather than a drift. It is a taste number, not a risk price.
        //
        // The dirt stages are still what feed the decoration pass: floor_growth and hanging_growth
        // both key off dirt, so thinning dirt in favour of air costs growth.
        //
        // ONE PLACE STILL DOES NOT OPEN, deliberately: a PIT's faces. RoomPitGenerator lines every
        // cut face unconditionally, because a sheer shaft that opens along its side pours a cavern
        // into the room rather than showing a patch of earth underfoot. A hole in a floor is a hole
        // in a surface a player stands on; a hole in a pit wall is a hole in the room's shell.
        // ===================================================================================
        {
          "surface": "floor",
          "block": "minecraft:cobblestone",
          "output_blocks": [
            { "block": "minecraft:mossy_cobblestone", "probability": 0.15 },
            { "block": "dungeonblocks:mossy_rubble", "probability": 0.25 },
            { "block": "minecraft:dirt", "probability": 0.25 }
          ]
        },
        // THE SECOND ROUTE OFF THE SAME BLOCK, AND IT IS A FALLTHROUGH, NOT A FORK (Gottsch,
        // 2026-08-29). SurfaceAgingProcessor tries a block's rules in AUTHORED ORDER against one
        // random seeded per position; a rule takes only if its FIRST stage hits, and a miss falls
        // through to the next. So this rule is only ever offered the 85% the route above declined,
        // and its authored number is not the share of floor it gets.
        //
        // 0.1765 IS 0.15/0.85, which is what actually lands on 15% of the floor -- an even split
        // with the route above, and the same 30% total the chain decayed at when it was one route.
        // Authoring 0.15 here looks even and is not: measured, it gives 15% / 12.75%.
        //
        // The k-th rule listed on one block wants s / (1 - (k-1)s) for share s, so re-derive this
        // if the route above changes. The split is of ENTRY: what SURVIVES also depends on each
        // route's later stages.
        {
          "surface": "floor",
          "block": "minecraft:cobblestone",
          "output_blocks": [
            { "block": "dungeonblocks:rubble", "probability": 0.1765 },
            { "block": "minecraft:dirt", "probability": 0.25 },
            { "block": "minecraft:air", "probability": 0.15 }
          ]
        },
        // The floor's OTHER two materials. `packed_mud` is the speckle accent in a room and the
        // corridor's own floor; `mud_bricks` is the corridor's alternate. Without these a corridor
        // floor would be inert -- the mud_bricks chain below is gated to the walls, so it does not
        // reach a floor any more.
        {
          "surface": "floor",
          "block": "minecraft:packed_mud",
          "output_blocks": [
            { "block": "minecraft:dirt", "probability": 0.25 }
          ]
        },
        {
          "surface": "floor",
          "block": "minecraft:mud_bricks",
          "output_blocks": [
            { "block": "minecraft:packed_mud", "probability": 0.35 },
            { "block": "minecraft:dirt", "probability": 0.25 }
          ]
        },
        {
          "surface": "floor",
          "block": "dungeonblocks:large_bricks",
          "output_blocks": [
            { "block": "minecraft:bricks", "probability": 0.35 },
            { "block": "dungeonblocks:cobblestone_brick", "probability": 0.3 },
            { "block": "dungeonblocks:gravel_brick", "probability": 0.15 }
          ]
        },
        // ===================================================================================
        // ABOVE THE FLOOR -- walls, ceiling, and anything standing in the room.
        //
        // Unchanged from what this file shipped with, now that it can be said to apply to the
        // walls only:
        //
        //     mud_bricks -> packed_mud -> dirt -> air
        //     packed_mud  21%      dirt  7.65%      air  1.35%
        //
        // Raise the FIRST stage to make the depth more ruinous; raise the LAST to trade crumbled
        // earth for open holes.
        //
        // AIR IS THE TERMINUS, AND IT DOES BREACH THE SHELL SOMETIMES. A processor sees the block,
        // never the surface -- which used to mean the air stage that is a welcome hole in a wall
        // was the same rule as a hole in a FLOOR. It is not any more: this rule is gated to the
        // walls and the ceiling, and the floor chains above stop at dirt. What has not changed is
        // that a hole in an OUTER wall lets the terrain behind it in, water included.
        //
        // MARK ACCEPTED THAT ON 2026-08-25: if it leaves a hole, it leaves a hole. A dungeon
        // breached by the ground around it is the look, and the occasional wet room is its price.
        // 1.35% is a taste decision, not a risk price.
        //
        // What it buys, besides holes: `unsupported` in the decoration pass below can only fire
        // where weathering produced air, and floor_growth/hanging_growth need the dirt stage to
        // sprout from.
        // ===================================================================================
        {
          "surface": "above_floor",
          "block": "minecraft:cobblestone",
          "output_blocks": [
            { "block": "minecraft:mossy_cobblestone", "probability": 0.15 },
            { "block": "dungeonblocks:mossy_rubble", "probability": 0.25 },
            { "block": "minecraft:air", "probability": 0.125 }
          ]
        },
        {
          "surface": "above_floor",
          "block": "minecraft:cobblestone",
          "output_blocks": [
            { "block": "dungeonblocks:rubble", "probability": 0.1765 },
            { "block": "minecraft:dirt", "probability": 0.25 },
            { "block": "minecraft:air", "probability": 0.125 }
          ]
        },
        {
          "surface": "above_floor",
          "block": "minecraft:mud_bricks",
          "output_blocks": [
            { "block": "minecraft:packed_mud", "probability": 0.3 },
            { "block": "minecraft:dirt", "probability": 0.3 },
            { "block": "minecraft:air", "probability": 0.15 }
          ]
        },
        {
          "surface": "above_floor",
          "block": "minecraft:mud_brick_stairs",
          "output_blocks": [
            { "block": "minecraft:air", "probability": 0.1 }
          ]
        },
        {
          "surface": "above_floor",
          "block": "dungeonblocks:square_mud_brick",
          "output_blocks": [
            { "block": "minecraft:packed_mud", "probability": 0.3 },
            { "block": "minecraft:dirt", "probability": 0.25 },
            { "block": "minecraft:air", "probability": 0.15 }
          ]
        },
        // SIX RULES BELOW AND ABOVE CANNOT FIRE ON FLOOR 0 TODAY, AND THAT IS A DECISION (Mark,
        // 2026-09-06). `square_mud_brick_stairs`, `mud_brick_slab`, the two
        // `square_mud_brick_*facade_block`s, `large_bricks` and `bricks` have no rule-firing path
        // right now: the mud band's palette places `mud_bricks`, `square_mud_brick`,
        // `mud_brick_stairs`, `packed_mud` and `mossy_square_mud_brick`, and none of these six.
        //
        // They are KEPT, not deleted. The aging was written ahead of the palette for the full mud
        // vocabulary, and the palette is the half still expected to grow -- deleting the rules now
        // would mean re-deriving them later from nothing. Do NOT report them as #26 "aging rules
        // that can never fire"; that sweep is for rules whose input the motif can never place, and
        // these are waiting on authoring that has not happened yet.
        //
        // The one to actually look at, if anyone revisits this: `minecraft:bricks` is both an input
        // here and an OUTPUT of another rule in this file. The AgingProcessor does not chain across
        // rules within a pass, so it is very likely harmless -- but it is the exact shape of the
        // two-routes-on-one-block fallthrough, so confirm rather than assume.
        {
          "surface": "above_floor",
          "block": "dungeonblocks:square_mud_brick_stairs",
          "output_blocks": [
            { "block": "minecraft:air", "probability": 0.1 }
          ]
        },
        {
          "surface": "above_floor",
          "block": "dungeonblocks:square_mud_brick_facade_block",
          "output_blocks": [
            { "block": "minecraft:air", "probability": 0.1 }
          ]
        },
        {
          "surface": "above_floor",
          "block": "dungeonblocks:square_mud_brick_quarter_facade_block",
          "output_blocks": [
            { "block": "minecraft:air", "probability": 0.1 }
          ]
        },

        // THE ARCHES, which are stone on a mud floor and were the last pristine thing down here.
        //
        // Floor 0's arch is stone brick, not mud, and that is not an oversight to correct in the
        // palette: `archBlock` on a band is INERT for classic, because classic authors `styles` and
        // the arch lives inside the style the planner rolled. So the mud band gets mud floors and
        // ceilings with STONE-BRICK ARCHES, by design -- and until these rules existed, those
        // arches were the only unweathered geometry on an otherwise aged floor. Backlog #45 step 4.
        //
        // They age as STONE, not as mud. The blocks really are stone brick, and a stone arch that
        // crumbled to air the way the mud shapes above do would read as the wrong material failing.
        // What the band changes is the DIRECTION of the decay: this is the shallow, wet stratum, so
        // the mossy stage is weighted above the stone list's 0.3 and the dry andesite stage the
        // stone list uses is dropped -- polished andesite is an arid, igneous read and wrong here.
        //
        // Two rules on `stone_brick_stairs` rather than one, matching the stone list: a rule is a
        // single independent roll, so staging is what gives the compound outcome. They do NOT chain
        // within a pass -- the AgingProcessor does not re-age its own output -- so a stair turned to
        // cobblestone by the second rule is not then taken by the third rule below in the same pass.
        {
          "surface": "any",
          "block": "minecraft:stone_brick_stairs",
          "output_blocks": [
            { "block": "minecraft:mossy_stone_brick_stairs", "probability": 0.35 },
            { "block": "minecraft:mossy_cobblestone_stairs", "probability": 0.35 }
          ]
        },
        {
          "surface": "any",
          "block": "minecraft:stone_brick_stairs",
          "output_blocks": [
            { "block": "minecraft:cobblestone_stairs", "probability": 0.43 },
            // The shape-break to dirt is DELIBERATE and is kept at the stone list's rate. On this
            // floor it reads as an arch foot sinking into the mud, which is the one place in the
            // dungeon where losing the stair shape entirely is the right picture.
            { "block": "minecraft:dirt", "probability": 0.05 }
          ]
        },
        {
          "surface": "any",
          "block": "minecraft:cobblestone_stairs",
          "output_blocks": [
            { "block": "minecraft:mossy_cobblestone_stairs", "probability": 0.3 },
            { "block": "dungeonblocks:rubble", "probability": 0.05 }
          ]
        },
        {
          "surface": "above_floor",
          "block": "minecraft:mud_brick_slab",
          "output_blocks": [
            { "block": "minecraft:air", "probability": 0.1 }
          ]
        },
        {
          "surface": "above_floor",
          "block": "dungeonblocks:left_large_stone_brick",
          "output_blocks": [
            { "block": "dungeonblocks:mossy_left_large_stone_brick", "probability": 0.3 },
            { "block": "dungeonblocks:rubble", "probability": 0.3 },
            { "block": "minecraft:dirt", "probability": 0.15 }
          ]
        },
        {
          "surface": "above_floor",
          "block": "dungeonblocks:right_large_stone_brick",
          "output_blocks": [
            { "block": "dungeonblocks:mossy_right_large_stone_brick", "probability": 0.3 },
            { "block": "dungeonblocks:rubble", "probability": 0.3 },
            { "block": "minecraft:dirt", "probability": 0.15 }
          ]
        },
        {
          "surface": "above_floor",
          "block": "dungeonblocks:square_stone_brick",
          "output_blocks": [
            { "block": "dungeonblocks:mossy_square_stone_brick", "probability": 0.3 },
            { "block": "dungeonblocks:rubble", "probability": 0.3 },
            { "block": "minecraft:dirt", "probability": 0.15 }
          ]
        },
        {
          "surface": "above_floor",
          "block": "dungeonblocks:left_large_mud_brick",
          "output_blocks": [
            { "block": "dungeonblocks:mossy_left_large_mud_brick", "probability": 0.3 },
            { "block": "minecraft:packed_mud", "probability": 0.3 },
            { "block": "minecraft:dirt", "probability": 0.15 }
          ]
        },
        {
          "surface": "above_floor",
          "block": "dungeonblocks:right_large_mud_brick",
          "output_blocks": [
            { "block": "dungeonblocks:mossy_right_large_mud_brick", "probability": 0.3 },
            { "block": "minecraft:packed_mud", "probability": 0.3 },
            { "block": "minecraft:dirt", "probability": 0.15 }
          ]
        },
        {
          "surface": "above_floor",
          "block": "dungeonblocks:large_bricks",
          "output_blocks": [
            { "block": "minecraft:bricks", "probability": 0.3 },
            { "block": "dungeonblocks:cobblestone_brick", "probability": 0.3 },
            { "block": "dungeonblocks:gravel_brick", "probability": 0.15 }
          ]
        },
        {
          "surface": "above_floor",
          "block": "minecraft:bricks",
          "output_blocks": [
            { "block": "dungeonblocks:cobblestone_brick", "probability": 0.3 },
            { "block": "dungeonblocks:gravel_brick", "probability": 0.3 },
            { "block": "minecraft:dirt", "probability": 0.15 }
          ]
        },

        // =====================================================================================
        // #45 STEP 4, 2026-08-31. The band's schemes grew a stone-and-timber vocabulary on
        // 2026-08-30/31 and this file had rules for none of it -- so a floor-0 room showed
        // cracked, mossy, half-crumbled mud walls with pristine stone trim standing in front of
        // them. Exactly #37's complaint, one band down. Measured: 11 of the 18 block ids the band
        // places had no rule here.
        //
        // ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
        // THE TIMBER IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT, AND THAT IS THE INTERESTING DECISION (Mark,
        // 2026-08-31). There is NO minecraft:spruce_log rule in this file and there should not
        // be one.
        //
        // The mechanical problem came first. Classic ages spruce_log 15% straight to air, and its
        // own note says why that is safe THERE: spruce is only ever a beam or a bracket, a beam
        // hangs below the ceiling slab, so a decayed one leaves a gap in a run with the shell
        // intact. That note also warns: "Move spruce_log into a wall or ceiling and that
        // distinction is lost." The mud band moved it into PILLARS -- five authored uses across
        // mud_timber_pillars, both pilastered schemes and the centre pier -- and the same rule
        // would punch holes through the middle of a post, which hangs rather than reads as aged.
        //
        // NOTHING CAN SEPARATE THE TWO. PieceSurface offers `any`, `floor` and `above_floor`, and
        // a post and a ceiling beam are both above_floor. That is #15 exactly, and it is why this
        // could not be solved with a rate.
        //
        // So the absence became the story instead: the mud stratum is the dungeon being reclaimed
        // from underneath, and the timber is SHORING someone put in recently to hold it up. Fresh
        // wood against rotten brick reads as repair, not as an oversight -- which is the one
        // reading that makes untouched timber correct rather than merely tolerable. Anything
        // spruce is new; anything stone is old.
        //
        // BOTH CORBELS AGE, THE LOGS DO NOT -- the split is by ELEMENT, not by material (Mark,
        // 2026-08-31). Written down because the material split is the obvious reading and it is
        // the wrong one: "anything spruce is new" would have spared the spruce bracket too.
        //
        // The line that actually matters is the one classic already draws between a bracket and a
        // run: a corbel is a SINGLE CELL and reads as missing, where a post is a run and a hole in
        // the middle of it hangs. So a bracket may rot away at 10% wherever it is made of, and no
        // log may, however decorative its position -- because nothing downstream can tell which
        // logs are posts. The shoring reads as shoring whose brackets have gone here and there,
        // which is a better picture than shoring nothing has touched at all.
        //
        // stripped_spruce_log stays unaged for a second, unrelated reason it already had in
        // classic: eight templates choose it deliberately, so a rule would age an author's choice.
        // ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
        {
          // The end pilasters' shaft. Classic's chain adapted to this band's palette: it goes to
          // cobblestone rather than to cracked brick, because cobblestone is what this depth is
          // already losing its masonry to everywhere else in this file.
          "surface": "above_floor",
          "block": "minecraft:stone_bricks",
          "output_blocks": [
            { "block": "minecraft:mossy_stone_bricks", "probability": 0.3 },
            { "block": "dungeonblocks:rubble", "probability": 0.3 },
            { "block": "minecraft:dirt", "probability": 0.15 }
          ]
        },
        {
          // The one-block plinth in `plain` (the size-1 dais option, small rooms only), which this
          // band inherits from base.json along with the 3x3 dais above it. Classic's own slab rule
          // verbatim, and `any` for the same reason it uses `any`: a plinth is one cell that reads
          // as floor from above and as a step from the side, so gating it on a surface would leave
          // it pristine half the time.
          //
          // NOT taken to `dungeonblocks:rubble` or dirt the way this band's full blocks are: those
          // are full cubes, and a slab that decays into one grows. The shape has to survive the
          // decay even where the material does not.
          "surface": "any",
          "block": "minecraft:stone_brick_slab",
          "output_blocks": [
            { "block": "minecraft:mossy_stone_brick_slab", "probability": 0.3 },
            { "block": "minecraft:cobblestone_slab", "probability": 0.3 }
          ]
        },
        {
          // The joist bracket in `joisted_hall`. A missing bracket under an intact beam is the
          // right picture for this band: the timber was laid into corbels that were already there.
          //
          // Straight to air at 10%, copying classic's spruce corbel rather than inventing a rate,
          // and for the reason classic gives: a bracket is a single cell and reads as MISSING,
          // where a run reads as broken.
          "surface": "above_floor",
          "block": "dungeonblocks:stone_corbel_block",
          "output_blocks": [
            { "block": "minecraft:air", "probability": 0.1 }
          ]
        },
        {
          // The bracket under `mud_timber_pillars`' joists. Same 10% as the stone one above and as
          // classic's own -- one rate for the element, whatever it is made of. It is the only
          // SPRUCE block this file ages, and the note above is why that is not a contradiction.
          "surface": "above_floor",
          "block": "dungeonblocks:spruce_corbel_block",
          "output_blocks": [
            { "block": "minecraft:air", "probability": 0.1 }
          ]
        },
        // ===================================================================================
        // DARK IRON -- the rust ladder. dungeonblocks cuts three aged stages for the grate and
        // the heavy trapdoor and for nothing else in the dark iron family, so these two blocks
        // get a chain and the plate brackets get none: tarnished (a dulled sheen), rusted
        // (orange bloom), corroded (eaten through and pitted).
        //
        // IT NEVER REACHES AIR, unlike the chain rules beside it. Both blocks are things the
        // player stands on or opens, so a missing one is a hole to fall through rather than a
        // bit of decay to walk past. Corroded is the end of the ladder on purpose.
        //
        // Composed over the three stages: tarnished on ~40% of grates, rusted on ~14%, corroded
        // on ~3.5%. Retune the first number to move the whole ladder.
        // ===================================================================================
        {
          "surface": "any",
          "block": "dungeonblocks:dark_iron_grate",
          "output_blocks": [
            { "block": "dungeonblocks:tarnished_dark_iron_grate", "probability": 0.4 },
            { "block": "dungeonblocks:rusted_dark_iron_grate", "probability": 0.35 },
            { "block": "dungeonblocks:corroded_dark_iron_grate", "probability": 0.25 }
          ]
        },
        {
          "surface": "any",
          "block": "dungeonblocks:dark_iron_heavy_trapdoor",
          "output_blocks": [
            { "block": "dungeonblocks:tarnished_dark_iron_heavy_trapdoor", "probability": 0.4 },
            { "block": "dungeonblocks:rusted_dark_iron_heavy_trapdoor", "probability": 0.35 },
            { "block": "dungeonblocks:corroded_dark_iron_heavy_trapdoor", "probability": 0.25 }
          ]
        },

        // ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
        // CHAINS (added 2026-09-07). The mud band's hallways hang the same lantern runs the dry
        // ones do -- minecraft:chain under a dungeonblocks:dungeon_lantern -- and they weather at
        // the same 10% a link. One rate for the element, whatever band it is in, which is the
        // same call the corbel above makes.
        //
        // The reasoning lives on the matching rules in classic_weathering: read it there before
        // retuning either file. The short version is that a link is not a brick, because
        // everything under a missing link is left hanging from nothing, and the
        // dungeons2:hanging_sweep entry lower down is what makes these rules safe to have at all.
        //
        // `any`, unlike almost everything else in this file. A chain occupies the open cells
        // BELOW the ceiling, so it is neither a ceiling surface nor `above_floor` in any useful
        // sense, and nothing else in the dungeon is made of chain -- the block alone identifies
        // the element, which is the #15 problem not applying for once.
        //
        // swinging_chain is listed even though no mud template uses one yet: the two rules travel
        // together, and a rule for a block the band never places is inert, not wrong.
        // ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
        {
          "surface": "any",
          "block": "minecraft:chain",
          "output_blocks": [
            { "block": "minecraft:air", "probability": 0.1 }
          ]
        },
        {
          "surface": "any",
          "block": "dungeonblocks:swinging_chain",
          "output_blocks": [
            { "block": "minecraft:air", "probability": 0.1 }
          ]
        },

        // ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
        // THE STONE PAVING, floor-scoped. `mud_diamond_inlay`'s cross, the pilastered scheme's
        // border footing and `joisted_hall`'s checkerboard all lay dressed stone underfoot, and
        // dungeonblocks:square_stone_brick above is scoped `above_floor` -- so the same block
        // aged on a wall and stayed pristine on the floor beside it.
        //
        // These stop at dirt and never reach air, matching the packed_mud and mud_bricks floor
        // chains above. A hole underfoot opens onto raw terrain rather than onto the look the
        // wall holes give.
        // ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
        {
          "surface": "floor",
          "block": "dungeonblocks:square_stone_brick",
          "output_blocks": [
            { "block": "dungeonblocks:mossy_square_stone_brick", "probability": 0.3 },
            { "block": "dungeonblocks:rubble", "probability": 0.25 },
            { "block": "minecraft:dirt", "probability": 0.25 }
          ]
        },
        {
          "surface": "floor",
          "block": "minecraft:stone_bricks",
          "output_blocks": [
            { "block": "minecraft:mossy_stone_bricks", "probability": 0.3 },
            { "block": "dungeonblocks:rubble", "probability": 0.25 },
            { "block": "minecraft:dirt", "probability": 0.25 }
          ]
        },
        {
          // The checkerboard's second material. Plain stone has no dressed form to decay INTO, so
          // it goes straight to the rubble stage the dressed blocks reach second.
          "surface": "floor",
          "block": "minecraft:stone",
          "output_blocks": [
            { "block": "minecraft:cobblestone", "probability": 0.3 },
            { "block": "dungeonblocks:rubble", "probability": 0.25 },
            { "block": "minecraft:dirt", "probability": 0.25 }
          ]
        },
        {
          // The border's corner and edge blocks. Neither has an aged variant of its own -- there
          // is no mossy chiseled brick and no worn polished andesite -- so both drop to
          // cobblestone first, which is this band's universal "was dressed, now is not".
          "surface": "floor",
          "block": "minecraft:chiseled_stone_bricks",
          "output_blocks": [
            { "block": "minecraft:cobblestone", "probability": 0.25 },
            { "block": "dungeonblocks:rubble", "probability": 0.25 },
            { "block": "minecraft:dirt", "probability": 0.25 }
          ]
        },
        {
          "surface": "floor",
          "block": "minecraft:polished_andesite",
          "output_blocks": [
            { "block": "minecraft:cobblestone", "probability": 0.25 },
            { "block": "dungeonblocks:rubble", "probability": 0.25 },
            { "block": "minecraft:dirt", "probability": 0.25 }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      // dungeons2:decoration -- the NEIGHBOUR-aware pass. Everything above decides one
      // block at a time; this one decides from a block's surroundings, which is what
      // cobwebs (air with a wall beside it) and creeping growth need.
      //
      // Its probabilities are ABSOLUTE, not conditional -- one roll per candidate
      // position, unlike the rule/aging chains above.
      //
      // It only ever writes into a cell the piece itself places as AIR (bar underwater
      // growth, which replaces the piece's own water). Nothing here can touch terrain.
      "processor_type": "dungeons2:decoration",

      // Air with at least one horizontally adjacent solid block. Corners and wall faces
      // web up; the middle of a room stays clear.
      "cobwebs": {
        "probability": 0.02,
        "blocks": ["minecraft:cobweb"]
      },

      // The angle webs are a DIFFERENT behaviour, not more entries in the palette above.
      // They are modelled as a triangular sheet gathering into the angle where two
      // surfaces meet and tapering away from it, so they need a wall AND a floor or
      // ceiling -- one halfway up a bare wall has nothing to gather into and reads as a
      // sheet hanging in mid-air. Both surfaces must be full cubes for the same reason
      // wall growth needs one; the processor works out the facing from the neighbours,
      // and picks the junction with the block's `half` (top = ceiling, bottom = floor).
      //
      // Higher than the plain web's rate on purpose: this fires on far fewer cells --
      // only wall/floor and wall/ceiling junctions -- so 0.02 here would be nearly
      // invisible.
      "corner_cobwebs": {
        "probability": 0.06,
        "blocks": [
          "dungeonblocks:angle_cobweb_1",
          "dungeonblocks:angle_cobweb_2"
        ]
      },

      // Growth in the air beside a wall, attached to the wall's face. "probability" is
      // the chance with nothing already growing next to the spot; each of the 6
      // neighbours that IS growth adds "bonus", capped at "max", and the spot inherits
      // its neighbour's species. That's what makes it spread in patches rather than an
      // even speckle -- turn "bonus" up for bigger, blotchier patches. Blocks should be
      // multiface (glow-lichen-like) to take a face; anything else is placed as-is.
      "wall_growth": {
        "probability": 0.04,
        "bonus": 0.22,
        "max": 0.55,
        "blocks": [
          "minecraft:glow_lichen",
          "dungeonblocks:lichen",
          "dungeonblocks:mold"
        ]
      },

      // What counts as dirt for floor_growth/hanging_growth below. Classic dungeons are
      // not authored with dirt -- this catches the dirt the AGING processor above makes
      // out of decayed stairs, which is exactly where a tuft of growth belongs.
      "dirt": {
        "tags": ["minecraft:dirt"]
      },
      // On top of a dirt block, where the cell above it is air. All single-block plants that
      // survive in the dark: mushrooms need darkness, and grass/fern/moss only need light to
      // SPREAD, not to stay put, so none of these pop off in an unlit dungeon.
      // (No tall_grass or large_fern -- those are two blocks high and only the lower half
      // would be placed.)
      //
      // The two "entity" entries are MOBS grown as if they were plants. GMM's Shrieker and Violet
      // Fungus root themselves and never move, so a patch of decayed dirt is exactly where they
      // belong -- and dungeons2:decoration spawns them from its finalizeProcessing pass, which has
      // a real ServerLevelAccessor and the chunk box to dedupe against. No marker block involved.
      //
      // THE PALETTE IS WEIGHTED. An entry is either a bare id (weight 1) or {block, weight}, and
      // the draw is one roll over the total. Weights matter here more than anywhere else in this
      // file: unweighted, the two fungi were 2 of 8 entries -- a QUARTER of everything that grows,
      // which is ground-cover frequency for something that is actually a monster. At 1 each against
      // the mushrooms' 10 they are 2 of 62, about 3%.
      "floor_growth": {
        "probability": 0.35,
        "blocks": [
          { "block": "minecraft:brown_mushroom", "weight": 10 },
          { "block": "minecraft:red_mushroom", "weight": 10 },
          { "block": "minecraft:moss_carpet", "weight": 10 },
          { "block": "minecraft:grass", "weight": 10 },
          { "block": "minecraft:fern", "weight": 10 },
          { "block": "minecraft:dead_bush", "weight": 10 },
          { "entity": "dungeons2:shrieker", "weight": 1 },
          { "entity": "dungeons2:violet_fungus", "weight": 1 }
        ]
      },
      // Underneath a dirt block, where the cell below it is air.
      "hanging_growth": {
        "probability": 0.3,
        "blocks": ["minecraft:hanging_roots"]
      },

      // Architectural blocks that delete themselves once the wall behind them is gone --
      // a corbel or ledge left hanging in the air after what it was bracketed onto
      // crumbled away. Structural, so no probability: it either has a wall or it doesn't.
      //
      // A block with a "facing" property is held up from BEHIND and by nothing else: it
      // GIVES support to what sits on top of it, it doesn't TAKE support from there, nor
      // from below, nor from whatever happens to sit beside it. Only the neighbour at
      // facing.getOpposite() is consulted.
      //
      // Deliberately generous otherwise, since a false positive deletes authored
      // architecture: any non-air block behind counts (a ledge mounted on a stair or on
      // another ledge stays put), and so does a neighbour that simply isn't part of this
      // piece -- absent means "nothing placed here", not "nothing is here".
      //
      // It follows that this fires only where weathering actually produced air, so how
      // often you see it is really a property of the aging rules above.
      "unsupported": {
        "tags": [
          "dungeonblocks:corbels",
          "dungeonblocks:ledges"
        ]
      },

      // Water standing on a solid floor becomes seagrass. Only the piece's OWN water: the
      // mud sewer rooms author it. Aquifer water that seeps in after generation is not in
      // the block list and gets nothing.
      "underwater_growth": {
        "probability": 0.15,
        "blocks": ["minecraft:seagrass"]
      }
      // Lily pads on the air above water are available but OFF:
      //   "floating_growth":   { "probability": 0.02, "blocks": ["minecraft:lily_pad"] }
    },
    // =========================================================================================
    // dungeons2:pot (added 2026-08-29) -- backlog #56. Turns an authored dungeons2:pot_marker into
    // pot ENTITIES and leaves air where the marker stood.
    //
    // BEFORE the sweep, deliberately. This processor's finalizeProcessing rewrites each marker cell
    // to air; decoration_sweep's job is to remove decoration left unsupported by a cell that is now
    // empty, so it has to run after the cell empties or it sweeps against stale ground.
    //
    // No loot_table or variant here: those are pool-level fallbacks for markers that name none of
    // their own, and every marker is expected to. A marker that resolves to no variant logs
    // [D2-POT] and spawns nothing rather than guessing a pot.
    // =========================================================================================
    {
      "processor_type": "dungeons2:pot"
    },

    // =========================================================================================
    // dungeons2:chest (added 2026-08-30) -- backlog #61, wiring the #48 step 3 marker that had
    // been left reachable from nothing. Turns an authored dungeons2:chest_marker into a chest
    // holding a loot table, and is the authored twin of the `chests` scheme slot.
    //
    // WHY IT IS HERE AND NOT IN ITS OWN LIST. It shipped in dungeons2:classic_chests, which no
    // pool ever referenced -- that list existed for one throwaway test template (0bc6a45) that was
    // removed again (93baad8), leaving the processor orphaned. Every room, entrance and transition
    // pool names a weathering list, so a marker processor that is not IN one is a marker block
    // left standing in a finished dungeon. Same conclusion #56 reached for pots, same placement.
    //
    // BEFORE the sweep, for the same reason the pot entry is: nothing it does should be judged
    // against ground a later pass is still going to change.
    //
    // Unlike the pot entry this one DOES carry a pool-level default, because "an ordinary chest
    // with ordinary dungeon loot" is a meaningful thing to want and a pot's variant is not -- a
    // pot with no variant has no appearance to fall back on. A marker's own `lootTable` NBT still
    // wins over this; the list is what a template full of ordinary chests relies on.
    //
    // Shallow-weighted, matching the motif's floor-0 band verbatim: this list is the mud
    // stratum's, and the mud stratum is where a dungeon starts. The main classic list carries a
    // deeper blend for the same reason.
    // =========================================================================================
    {
      "processor_type": "dungeons2:chest",
      "loot_tables": [
        { "loot_table": "dungeons2:chests/classic_shallow", "weight": 9 },
        { "loot_table": "dungeons2:chests/classic_deep", "weight": 1 }
      ]
    },

    // =========================================================================================
    // dungeons2:decoration_sweep (added 2026-08-26) -- undoes the decoration above in cells a
    // LATER piece is about to invalidate. It must come after dungeons2:decoration: it inspects
    // what that pass decided, and finalizeProcessing runs the list in order.
    //
    // THE DEFECT. Found at -38 1 1801: a dungeonblocks:mold with south=true clinging to a
    // polished_andesite_quarter_facade_block -- a quarter slab, so the quad hung 12/16 of a block
    // off its only support. wall_growth's isFullCube guard is what should have stopped that, and
    // it did: the piece north of that wall row grew the mold against ITS OWN full-cube wall block,
    // and the 5x11_hallway_2 prefab south of it -- authored, and therefore rendered last, per the
    // shared-wall rule in backlog #18 -- then re-skinned the same cell with the facade. The
    // processor validated inputs that were true when it ran and false by the time anyone saw them.
    //
    // WHY IT CANNOT BE FIXED INSIDE dungeons2:decoration. The piece that breaks the support is a
    // DIFFERENT piece running later; at growth time the facade does not exist yet, and a
    // piece-local processor is forbidden from reading the world anyway. Only the breaking piece is
    // in a position to notice, which is why this is a second entry rather than another rule.
    //
    // WHAT IT TOUCHES. Only cells this piece does NOT itself write (anything it writes is about to
    // be overwritten regardless), only blocks named below, and only when the support test that
    // placed them now fails. Anything it cannot see counts as supported -- a false positive
    // deletes somebody's architecture, a false negative is one mold quad that outstayed its
    // welcome.
    //
    // EVERY FIELD MIRRORS ONE dungeons2:decoration BEHAVIOUR and must name the same blocks it
    // does; a processor cannot see its siblings in the list, so the pairing is by convention and
    // pinned by DecorationSweepParityTest, which reads both entries out of this file.
    // (No underwater_growth counterpart: seagrass only replaces water the piece authored on its
    // own floor, so no later piece is placed to undercut it. floating_growth is off above.)
    // =========================================================================================
    {
      "processor_type": "dungeons2:decoration_sweep",

      // wall_growth. Multiface, so this repairs FACE BY FACE: a patch wrapping a corner keeps the
      // faces whose walls survived and loses only the dead one. Air when nothing is left.
      "growth": {
        "blocks": [
          "minecraft:glow_lichen",
          "dungeonblocks:lichen",
          "dungeonblocks:mold"
        ]
      },

      // cobwebs -- needs one horizontally adjacent solid block. Mostly a no-op in practice: a
      // facade still passes isSolid, so only a neighbour turning to AIR strands a plain web.
      "webs": {
        "blocks": ["minecraft:cobweb"]
      },

      // corner_cobwebs -- the pickier pair. Needs a full-cube wall behind its facing AND the
      // full-cube floor or ceiling its `half` names, exactly as maybeCornerCobweb required.
      "corner_webs": {
        "blocks": [
          "dungeonblocks:angle_cobweb_1",
          "dungeonblocks:angle_cobweb_2"
        ]
      },

      // Must be the same match as dungeons2:decoration's `dirt` -- it is what floor_growth and
      // hanging_growth below are tested against.
      "dirt": {
        "tags": ["minecraft:dirt"]
      },

      // floor_growth, minus the two entity entries: a Shrieker or a Violet Fungus is a MOB, and a
      // block sweep neither sees nor removes one.
      "floor_growth": {
        "blocks": [
          "minecraft:brown_mushroom",
          "minecraft:red_mushroom",
          "minecraft:moss_carpet",
          "minecraft:grass",
          "minecraft:fern",
          "minecraft:dead_bush"
        ]
      },
      "hanging_growth": {
        "blocks": ["minecraft:hanging_roots"]
      },

      // The same corbels and ledges the `unsupported` rule clears, re-tested once the neighbouring
      // piece has had its say: a ledge keeps whatever is behind its facing, and goes when that
      // turns to air.
      "unsupported": {
        "tags": [
          "dungeonblocks:corbels",
          "dungeonblocks:ledges"
        ]
      }
    },

    // =========================================================================================
    // dungeons2:hanging_sweep (added 2026-09-07) -- cuts a chain run loose below the link the
    // aging above just ate, and takes the lantern on the end of it with it.
    //
    // It is the second half of the chain rules and neither half works alone. A template writes
    // what it is told, so a link removed from the middle of a run leaves everything beneath it
    // hanging from nothing: a lit lantern floating in the middle of the room. Without this
    // entry the aging must be reverted, not merely retuned.
    //
    // AUTHORED AFTER EVERY AGING ENTRY, because finalizeProcessing runs the list in order and
    // the break it cascades from is what those left behind. Order against decoration and the
    // markers does not matter -- none of them touch a chain -- but it is placed after the
    // decoration sweep so that the two "undo what an earlier pass assumed" entries sit together.
    //
    // NOT dungeons2:support_sweep, and the two are not interchangeable. That one asks whether a
    // block is connected in ANY of six directions to something that reaches the ground,
    // specifically so a lintel over a door and a corbel bracketed onto a wall survive. A chain
    // is the opposite case: it is held at its ends and by nothing else, so six-way connectivity
    // would keep every severed run that happens to hang beside a wall -- which in these
    // hallways is most of them. Support here is vertical for that reason alone.
    //
    // `hanging` NAMES EVERY BLOCK IN THE RUN, links and fixture alike, because the run is
    // judged as a unit. The lanterns are listed even though nothing ages them: a
    // dungeonblocks:dungeon_lantern is what hangs on the end of a minecraft:chain, and unless
    // it is a member the sweep cuts the chain above it and leaves it floating -- the exact
    // artifact this exists to remove. minecraft:lantern and soul_lantern are here because
    // swinging_chain's own `fixture` enum offers them, so an author may reach for one next.
    //
    // A STANDING LANTERN IS SAFE. A run is anchored at EITHER end, so entrance_2's floor
    // lantern (hanging=false) is held by the cell beneath it and is never touched. That is why
    // the rule cannot simply be "look up": a BlockMatch names a BLOCK, and the standing and the
    // hanging lantern are the same block. The processor's own axis guard covers the other half
    // of that -- a minecraft:chain laid on its side is a bar, not a run, and is left alone.
    // =========================================================================================
    {
      "processor_type": "dungeons2:hanging_sweep",
      "hanging": {
        "blocks": [
          "minecraft:chain",
          "dungeonblocks:swinging_chain",
          "dungeonblocks:dungeon_lantern",
          "minecraft:lantern",
          "minecraft:soul_lantern"
        ]
      }
    },

    // =========================================================================================
    // dungeons2:spawner (Backlog #10, added 2026-08-14) -- swaps an authored
    // dungeons2:spawner_marker block for the invisible dungeons2:mob_set_spawner, which spawns
    // this set when a player comes within `proximity` blocks.
    //
    // IT MATCHES A BLOCK, NOT A `d2:spawner` DATA MARKER, and that is not a preference.
    // SinglePoolElement.getSettings installs BlockIgnoreProcessor.STRUCTURE_BLOCK BEFORE
    // appending this list, and that processor returns null for a structure block -- REMOVING it
    // rather than replacing it. A pool processor never sees one. The observed symptom was neither
    // a spawner nor a visible structure block but the terrain the dungeon was carved out of,
    // because the cell was never written at all. See JigsawStripsStructureBlocksTest.
    //
    // WHY IT LIVES IN THE WEATHERING LIST, despite not weathering anything: a pool element names
    // exactly ONE processor_list, and all four pools already name this one. Splitting it would
    // mean every pool JSON growing a second reference and every future motif remembering both.
    // The file is really "the classic motif's placement processors"; the name predates having
    // any that were not weathering.
    //
    // IT ONLY EVER FIRES ON AUTHORED TEMPLATES, since nothing procedural places the marker.
    // A procedural room reaches the same spawner through the motif config's `spawners` scheme
    // slot instead (RoomSpawnerGenerator -> BlockEntityData -> DungeonPiece.applyBlockEntity).
    // The two build the same block-entity tag; SpawnerTagParityTest is what keeps them agreeing,
    // so a field renamed here must be renamed there.
    //
    // EVERY FIELD BELOW IS A DEFAULT AN INDIVIDUAL MARKER MAY OVERRIDE (2026-09-03). The marker
    // carries a block entity, so a template can state mobSetName / proximity / minMobs / maxMobs /
    // type on one cell with /data merge block and leave the rest of its markers on these values.
    // This entry used to say "one marker = one set, a block carries no free text" -- that was
    // wrong: a structure template stores block-entity NBT PER CELL and hands it to a processor as
    // current.nbt(), which is how dungeons2:chest had been reading a per-marker loot table all
    // along. A second marker block via `marker_block` still works and is still the right answer
    // when a whole MOTIF wants a different default.
    // =========================================================================================
    {
      "processor_type": "dungeons2:spawner",
      // Required -- a spawner with no set is a block that does nothing, and defaulting it would
      // make that failure silent. Shipped set is the dungeon's own vermin (#40/#41).
      "mob_set": "dungeons2:classic_vermin",
      // Defaults to dungeons2:spawner_marker; stated explicitly because it is the authored half
      // of this contract and a template author has to know what to place.
      "marker_block": "dungeons2:spawner_marker",
      // Required, with no default anywhere -- see SpawnerConfig for why. 12 blocks: rooms run
      // 5-13 across, so this fires as the player comes through the door rather than once they
      // are well inside.
      "proximity": 12.0
    }
  ]
}
